6. Abstract
A heavily instrumentcd sub-scale model of a helicopter main rotor was tested in the NASA Lewis Research Centcr Icing Research Tunnel (IRT) in September and November 1989. The four-bladed main rotor had a diamctcr of 1.83 ni (6.00 ft) and the 0.124 m (4.9 in) chord rotor blades were specially fabricated for this experiment. The instrumented rotor was mounted on a Sikorsky Aircraft Powered Force Model, which enclosed a rotor balance and other measurement systems. The model rotor was exposed to a range of icing conditions that included variations in temperaturc, liquid water content, and median droplet diameter, and was opcrated over ranges of advance ratio, shaft angle, tip Mach number (rotor speed) and weight coefficient to determine the effect of these paranmeters on icc accretion. In addition to strain gage and balance data, the test was documented with still, vidco, and high spccd photography, ice profile tracings, and ice molds. This paper presents the sensitivity 01 thc model rotor to the test parameters, and compares the results to theoretical predictions. Test data quality was cxccllcnt, and icc accretion prediction methods and rotor performance prediction methods (using published icing lift and drag rclationships) reproduced thc performance trends obscrvcd in the test. Adjustments to the cnrrclatinn coefficients to itnprovc the level of correlation are suggested in this paper.
